# REVIEW-QA-2026-09-07.md, section 6, P2: this repository had no
# .gitattributes, so git's binary detection (which looks for a NUL byte
# early in the file) missed a ReportLab-written PDF that happens to carry
# none near its header, treated it as text, and applied line-ending
# conversion on checkout. The tree copy of the free print-and-play deck was
# 2,092 bytes larger than what production served, with the extra bytes
# being injected \r before every \n inside a compressed stream, corrupting
# it enough that a strict PDF reader could fail to open it. It only worked
# in production because every deploy so far has run from a Linux checkout;
# a Windows working tree (this business's own author's machine) would have
# shipped the corrupted copy the moment it committed one.
#
# Explicit binary declarations stop git from ever guessing wrong on these,
# regardless of which OS commits them.
*.pdf   binary
*.epub  binary
*.mobi  binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary

# Caption sidecars are compared byte for byte against what ops/video_srt.py
# produces, and that generator writes LF. Without this, git's autocrlf
# rewrites them to CRLF on checkout, so gate_srt_captions_current fails on
# every Windows working tree while passing in CI, and `git checkout` on these
# files silently breaks the gate until the generator is run again. Measured
# 2026-09-20: 114 files reported stale immediately after a checkout that
# changed nothing but line endings.
*.srt   text eol=lf

# Host config files that get copied to a Linux box verbatim. With
# core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout gives them CRLF, and logrotate does
# not parse a stanza whose lines end in ^M: it reports "Handling 0 logs" and
# rotates nothing, silently, for ever. Measured 2026-09-20 by installing one
# and dry-running it, which is the only reason it was caught before the log it
# guards existed.
ops/host/*   text eol=lf

# Third instance of the same class, 2026-09-24: ops/affiliate_report.py wrote
# affiliate-link-input-needed.csv with Python's csv.writer, whose RFC 4180
# default lineterminator is CRLF, while every other committed CSV in this
# repository (including its own sibling ops/build_manual_print.py's writer)
# is LF. Every regeneration showed all 124 rows as changed with no real
# content difference, which would mask a genuine edit inside the noise.
# Fixed at the generator (lineterminator="\n"); pinned here too so a future
# writer that forgets the same argument cannot reintroduce it silently.
*.csv   text eol=lf
